  "query_preview": "..\nToday through Monday: Ridging over the PNW will continue to promote \nwarmer than normal temperatures, clear skies, and dry conditions \nthrough Monday. Monday will be the warmest day of the week with high \ntemperatures in the mid 80s, around 10 degrees higher than normal \nfor this time of year. Onshore flow on Monday will contribute to a \nstrengthening west-east pressure gradient across the Cascades, \nwhich will yield breezy (10-15 mph wind gusts) afternoons for \nthe Cascade gaps. \n\nTuesday through Wednesday: An upper level low pressure system \nspinning in the Gulf of Alaska will move southward and sit off the \nthe British Columbia coast on Tuesday, brushing the forecast area as \nit does. The low will be too far north to yield any precipitation. \nHowever, the west-east pressure gradient across the state in \ngeneral will tighten and bring a broader increase in westerly \nwinds across the Inland Northwest. On Tuesday afternoon wind \ngusts in the Cascade gaps will be around 15-20 mph with some \nisolated 25 mph gusts on ridgetops. Additionally, temperatures \nwill cool down to near normal (low to mid 70s) by Wednesday. \n\nThursday through Sunday: After the low from Tuesday weakens, another \ntrough will drop down, this time moving further south and \nstrengthening off the PNW coast. It will then move inland and usher \nin a cold front on Friday, bringing a much more appreciable chance \nfor widespread rain through Saturday. While cluster analysis is \nunanimous on the low impacting the area, there is still uncertainty \nin the track and speed of the low. The deterministic GFS has the low \nmoving quicker and has the center of the low directly over \nWashington, whereas the ECMWF has the center of the low a bit \nfurther south and has it moving slower. If the GFS scenario pans \nout, this will bring more rain than the ECMWF scenario. \n\nWhile timing is uncertain, for now precipitation will start to move \neastward through the area Thursday afternoon and evening, and will \ncontinue through Saturday afternoon. Current NBM runs show the \nmajority of the forecast area receiving a tenth of an inch or \nmore of rain Thursday through Saturday. Areas that will see the \nhighest rainfall include the Cascades, northern mountains, Idaho\nPanhandle, and Palouse, which look to receive a quarter inch or\nmore of rain. Additionally, snow levels look to drop low enough\nto allow the highest elevations of the Cascades (6000-7000 feet\nand above) to see a couple inches of snow. Elevations 5000 feet\nand above will see under an inch of snow. We will keep a close \neye on snow levels through the week, as should any travelers or \nhikers anticipating being in the mountains this weekend. \n\nForcing associated with the incoming low combined with modest \ninstability (200-300 J/kg of SBCAPE) will support chances for \nthunderstorms Friday afternoon and evening. Areas that will see the \nhighest chances (10 to 15 percent) for thunderstorms are the \nnorthern mountains and northern Idaho Panhandle, while the broader \nareas receiving higher rainfall totals have a 5 to 10 percent \nchance. Current long range soundings indicate high PWATs (0.7 inches \nand above) alongside very saturated mid and low level relative \nhumidity values (90% and above). This indicates that main threats \nwith these thunderstorms will be isolated heavy rainfall rates. \n\nWinds will increase with the cold frontal passage Friday afternoon \nand evening, particularly across the Waterville Plateau and Columbia \nBasin area, where wind gusts 20-25 mph are expected. Elsewhere \nacross the Spokane area down to Pullman, wind gusts will be 10-15 \nmph.  \n\nBottom line: while aspects of the forecast are still uncertain, \nexpect unsettled weather at the end of the work week and weekend. \nMake sure to stay tuned as the forecast updates through the\nweek. /AS",
